@nonumber
# Act V

@nonumber
## Scene I

[italic: A room in the Garter Inn.]

[italic: Enter FALSTAFF and MISTRESS QUICKLY.]

[strong: Fal.] Prithee, no more prattling; go. I’ll hold. This is the third time; I hope good luck lies in odd numbers. Away! go. They say there is divinity in odd numbers, either in nativity, chance, or death. Away!

[strong: Quick.] I’ll provide you a chain; and I’ll do what I can to get you a pair of horns.

[strong: Fal.] Away, I say; time wears: hold up your head, and mince. \[[italic: Exit Mrs Quickly.]

[italic: Enter FORD.]

How now, Master Brook! Master Brook, the matter will be known to-night, or never. Be you in the Park about midnight, at Herne’s oak, and you shall see wonders.

[strong: Ford.] Went you not to her yesterday, sir, as you told me you had appointed?

[strong: Fal.] I went to her, Master Brook, as you see, like a poor old man: but I came from her, Master Brook, like a poor old woman. That same knave Ford, her husband, hath the finest mad devil of jealousy in him, Master Brook, that ever governed frenzy. I will tell you:—he beat me grievously, in the shape of a woman; for in the shape of man, Master Brook, I fear not Goliath with a weaver’s beam; because I know also life is a shuttle. I am in haste; go along with me: I’ll tell you all, Master Brook. Since I plucked geese, played truant, and whipped top, I knew not what ’twas to be beaten till lately. Follow me: I’ll tell you strange things of this knave Ford, on whom to-night I will be revenged, and I will deliver his wife into your hand. Follow. Strange things in hand, Master Brook! Follow. \[[italic: Exeunt.]

NOTES: V, 1

ACT V. SCENE I.] ACT IV. (continued). SCENE XII. Pope.

@nonumber
## Scene II

[italic: Windsor Park.]

[italic: Enter PAGE, SHALLOW, and SLENDER.]

[strong: Page.] Come, come; we’ll couch i’ the castle-ditch till we see the light of our fairies. Remember, son Slender, my daughter.

[strong: Slen.] Ay, forsooth; I have spoke with her, and we have a nay-word how to know one another: I come to her in white, and cry, ‘mum;’ she cries ‘budget;’ and by that we know one another.

[strong: Shal.] That’s good too: but what needs either your ‘mum’ or her ‘budget?’ the white will decipher her well enough. It hath struck ten o’clock.

[strong: Page.] The night is dark; light and spirits will become it well. Heaven prosper our sport! No man means evil but the devil, and we shall know him by his horns. Let’s away; follow me. \[[italic: Exeunt.]

NOTES: V, 2

SCENE II.] ACT V. SCENE I. Pope. Windsor Park] Pope. \[A street. Capell. 3: [italic: daughter]] om. F1 Q3.

@nonumber
## Scene III

[italic: A street leading to the Park.]

[italic: Enter MISTRESS PAGE, MISTRESS FORD, and DOCTOR CAIUS.]

[strong: Mrs Page.] Master doctor, my daughter is in green: when you see your time, take her by the hand, away with her to the deanery, and dispatch it quickly. Go before into the Park: we two must go together.

[strong: Caius.] I know vat I have to do. Adieu.

[strong: Mrs Page.] Fare you well, sir. \[[italic: Exit Caius.]] My husband will not rejoice so much at the abuse of Falstaff as he will chafe at the doctor’s marrying my daughter: but ’tis no matter; better a little chiding than a great deal of heart-break.

[strong: Mrs Ford.] Where is Nan now and her troop of fairies, and the Welsh devil Hugh?

[strong: Mrs Page.] They are all couched in a pit hard by Herne’s oak, with obscured lights; which, at the very instant of Falstaff’s and our meeting, they will at once display to the night.

[strong: Mrs Ford.] That cannot choose but amaze him.

[strong: Mrs Page.] If he be not amazed, he will be mocked; if he be amazed, he will every way be mocked.

[strong: Mrs Ford.] We’ll betray him finely.

@style verse
  [strong: Mrs Page.] Against such lewdsters and their lechery
  Those that betray them do no treachery.

[strong: Mrs Ford.] The hour draws on. To the oak, to the oak! \[[italic: Exeunt.]

NOTES: V, 3

SCENE III.] SCENE II. Pope. A street, &c.] \[Another street, &c. Capell.] 12: [italic: Hugh]] Capell. [italic: Herne] Ff Q3. [italic: Evans] Theobald (Thirlby conj.). 19: [italic: every way]] F1 Q3. om. F2 F3 F4.

@nonumber
## Scene IV

[italic: Windsor Park.]

[italic: Enter SIR HUGH EVANS disguised, with others as Fairies.]

[strong: Evans.] Trib, trib, fairies; come; and remember your parts: be pold, I pray you; follow me into the pit; and when I give the watch-’ords, do as I pid you: come, come; trib, trib. \[[italic: Exeunt.]

NOTES: V, 4

SCENE IV.] SCENE II. continued in Pope. 3: [italic: pid]] F1 Q3. [italic: bid] F2 F3 F4.

@nonumber
## Scene V

[italic: Another part of the Park.]

[italic: Enter FALSTAFF disguised as Horne.]

[strong: Fal.] The Windsor bell hath struck twelve; the minute draws on. Now, the hot-blooded gods assist me! Remember, Jove, thou wast a bull for thy Europa; love set on thy horns. O powerful love! that, in some respects, makes a beast a man; in some other, a man a beast. You were also, Jupiter, a swan for the love of Leda. O omnipotent Love! how near the god drew to the complexion of a goose!—A fault done first in the form of a beast;—O Jove, a beastly fault! And then another fault in the semblance of a fowl;—think on’t, Jove; a foul fault! When gods have hot backs, what shall poor men do? For me, I am here a Windsor stag; and the fattest, I think, i’ the forest. Send me a cool rut-time, Jove, or who can blame me to piss my tallow?—Who comes here? my doe?

[italic: Enter MISTRESS FORD and MISTRESS PAGE.]

[strong: Mrs Ford.] Sir John! art thou there, my deer? my male deer?

[strong: Fal.] My doe with the black scut! Let the sky rain potatoes; let it thunder to the tune of Green Sleeves, hail kissing-comfits, and snow eringoes; let there come a tempest of provocation, I will shelter me here.

[strong: Mrs Ford.] Mistress Page is come with me, sweetheart.

[strong: Fal.] Divide me like a bribe buck, each a haunch: I will keep my sides to myself, my shoulders for the fellow of this walk, and my horns I bequeath your husbands. Am I a woodman, ha? Speak I like Herne the hunter? Why, now is Cupid a child of conscience; he makes restitution. As I am a true spirit, welcome! \[[italic: Noise within.]

[strong: Mrs Page.] Alas, what noise?

[strong: Mrs Ford.] Heaven forgive our sins!

[strong: Fal.] What should this be?

[strong: Mrs Ford.] } Away, away! \[[italic: They run off.] [italic: Mrs Page.]}

[strong: Fal.] I think the devil will not have me damned, lest the oil that’s in me should set hell on fire; he would never else cross me thus.

[italic: Enter SIR HUGH EVANS, disguised as before; PISTOL, as Hobgoblin; MISTRESS QUICKLY, ANNE PAGE, and others, as Fairies, with tapers.]

@style verse
  [strong: Quick.] Fairies, black, grey, green, and white,
  You moonshine revellers, and shades of night,
  You orphan heirs of fixed destiny,
  Attend your office and your quality.
  Crier Hobgoblin, make the fairy oyes.

@style verse
  [strong: Pist.] Elves, list your names; silence, you airy toys.
  Cricket, to Windsor chimneys shalt thou leap:
  Where fires thou find’st unraked and hearths unswept,
  There pinch the maids as blue as bilberry:
  Our radiant queen hates sluts and sluttery.

@style verse
  [strong: Fal.] They are fairies; he that speaks to them shall die:
  I’ll wink and couch: no man their works must eye.
  \[[italic: Lies down upon his face.]

@style verse
  [strong: Evans.] Where’s Bede? Go you, and where you find a maid
  That, ere she sleep, has thrice her prayers said,
  Raise up the organs of her fantasy;
  Sleep she as sound as careless infancy:
  But those as sleep and think not on their sins,
  Pinch them, arms, legs, backs, shoulders, sides, and shins.

@style verse
  [strong: Quick.] About, about;
  Search Windsor Castle, elves, within and out:
  Strew good luck, ouphes, on every sacred room;
  That it may stand till the perpetual doom,
  In state as wholesome as in state ’tis fit,
  Worthy the owner, and the owner it.
  The several chairs of order look you scour
  With juice of balm and every precious flower:
  Each fair instalment, coat, and several crest,
  With loyal blazon, evermore be blest!
  And nightly, meadow-fairies, look you sing,
  Like to the Garter’s compass, in a ring:
  Th’ expressure that it bears, green let it be,
  More fertile-fresh than all the field to see;
  And [italic: Honi soit qui mal y pense] write
  In emerald tufts, flowers purple, blue, and white;
  Like sapphire, pearl, and rich embroidery,
  Buckled below fair knighthood’s bending knee:
  Fairies use flowers for their charactery.
  Away; disperse: but till ’tis one o’clock,
  Our dance of custom round about the oak
  Of Herne the hunter, let us not forget.

@style verse
  [strong: Evans.] Pray you, lock hand in hand; yourselves in order set;
  And twenty glow-worms shall our lanterns be,
  To guide our measure round about the tree.—
  But, stay; I smell a man of middle-earth.

[strong: Fal.] Heavens defend me from that Welsh fairy, lest he transform me to a piece of cheese!

[strong: Pist.] Vile worm, thou wast o’erlook’d even in thy birth.

@style verse
  [strong: Quick.] With trial-fire touch me his finger-end:
  If he be chaste, the flame will back descend,
  And turn him to no pain; but if he start,
  It is the flesh of a corrupted heart.

[strong: Pist.] A trial, come.

[strong: Evans.] Come, will this wood take fire?

\[[italic: They burn him with their tapers.]

[strong: Fal.] Oh, Oh, Oh!

@style verse
  [strong: Quick.] Corrupt, corrupt, and tainted in desire!
  About him, fairies; sing a scornful rhyme;
  And, as you trip, still pinch him to your time.

SONG.

Fie on sinful fantasy! Fie on lust and luxury! Lust is but a bloody fire, Kindled with unchaste desire, Fed in heart, whose flames aspire, As thoughts do blow them, higher and higher. Pinch him, fairies, mutually; Pinch him for his villany; Pinch him, and burn him, and turn him about, Till candles and starlight and moonshine be out.

[italic: During this song they pinch FALSTAFF. DOCTOR CAIUS comes one way, and steals away a boy in green; SLENDER another way, and takes off a boy in white; and FENTON comes, and] [italic: steals away Mrs ANNE PAGE. A noise of hunting is heard within. All the Fairies run away. FALSTAFF pulls off his buck’s head, and rises.]

[italic: Enter PAGE, FORD, MISTRESS PAGE and MISTRESS FORD.]

@style verse
  [strong: Page.] Nay, do not fly; I think we have watch’d you now:
  Will none but Herne the hunter serve your turn?

@style verse
  [strong: Mrs Page.] I pray you, come, hold up the jest no higher.
  Now, good Sir John, how like you Windsor wives?
  See you these, husband? do not these fair yokes
  Become the forest better than the town?

[strong: Ford.] Now, sir, who’s a cuckold now? Master Brook, Falstaff’s a knave, a cuckoldly knave; here are his horns, Master Brook: and, Master Brook, he hath enjoyed nothing of Ford’s but his buck-basket, his cudgel, and twenty pounds of money, which must be paid to Master Brook; his horses are arrested for it, Master Brook.

[strong: Mrs Ford.] Sir John, we have had ill luck; we could never meet. I will never take you for my love again; but I will always count you my deer.

[strong: Fal.] I do begin to perceive that I am made an ass.

[strong: Ford.] Ay, and an ox too: both the proofs are extant.

[strong: Fal.] And these are not fairies? I was three or four times in the thought they were not fairies: and yet the guiltiness of my mind, the sudden surprise of my powers, drove the grossness of the foppery into a received belief, in despite of the teeth of all rhyme and reason, that they were fairies. See now how wit may be made a Jack-a-Lent, when ’tis upon ill employment!

[strong: Evans.] Sir John Falstaff, serve Got, and leave your desires, and fairies will not pinse you.

[strong: Ford.] Well said, fairy Hugh.

[strong: Evans.] And leave you your jealousies too, I pray you.

[strong: Ford.] I will never mistrust my wife again, till thou art able to woo her in good English.

[strong: Fal.] Have I laid my brain in the sun, and dried it, that it wants matter to prevent so gross o’erreaching as this? Am I ridden with a Welsh goat too? shall I have a coxcomb of frize? Tis time I were choked with a piece of toasted cheese.

[strong: Evans.] Seese is not good to give putter; your pelly is all putter.

[strong: Fal.] ‘Seese’ and ’putter’! have I lived to stand at the taunt of one that makes fritters of English? This is enough to be the decay of lust and late-walking through the realm.

[strong: Mrs Page.] Why, Sir John, do you think, though we would have thrust virtue out of our hearts by the head and shoulders, and have given ourselves without scruple to hell, that ever the devil could have made you our delight?

[strong: Ford.] What, a hodge-pudding? a bag of flax?

[strong: Mrs Page.] A puffed man?

[strong: Page.] Old, cold, withered, and of intolerable entrails?

[strong: Ford.] And one that is as slanderous as Satan?

[strong: Page.] And as poor as Job?

[strong: Ford.] And as wicked as his wife?

[strong: Evans.] And given to fornications, and to taverns, and sack, and wine, and metheglins, and to drinkings, and swearings, and starings, pribbles and prabbles?

[strong: Fal.] Well, I am your theme: you have the start of me; I am dejected; I am not able to answer the Welsh flannel; ignorance itself is a plummet o’er me: use me as you will.

[strong: Ford.] Marry, sir, we’ll bring you to Windsor, to one Master Brook, that you have cozened of money, to whom you should have been a pander: over and above that you have suffered, I think to repay that money will be a biting affliction.

[strong: Page.] Yet be cheerful, knight: thou shalt eat a posset to-night at my house; where I will desire thee to laugh at my wife, that now laughs at thee: tell her Master Slender hath married her daughter.

[strong: Mrs Page.] \[[italic: Aside]] Doctors doubt that: if Anne Page be my daughter, she is, by this, Doctor Caius’ wife.

[italic: Enter SLENDER.]

[strong: Slen.] Whoa, ho! ho, father Page!

[strong: Page.] Son, how now! how now, son! have you dispatched?

[strong: Slen.] Dispatched!—I’ll make the best in Gloucestershire know on’t; would I were hanged, la, else!

[strong: Page.] Of what, son?

[strong: Slen.] I came yonder at Eton to marry Mistress Anne Page, and she’s a great lubberly boy. If it had not been i’ the church, I would have swinged him, or he should have swinged me. If I did not think it had been Anne Page, would I might never stir!—and ’tis a postmaster’s boy.

[strong: Page.] Upon my life, then, you took the wrong.

[strong: Slen.] What need you tell me that? I think so, when I took a boy for a girl. If I had been married to him, for all he was in woman’s apparel, I would not have had him.

[strong: Page.] Why, this is your own folly. Did not I tell you how you should know my daughter by her garments?

[strong: Slen.] I went to her in white, and cried ‘mum,’ and she cried ‘budget,’ as Anne and I had appointed; and yet it was not Anne, but a postmaster’s boy.

[strong: Mrs Page.] Good George, be not angry: I knew of your purpose; turned my daughter into green; and, indeed, she is now with the doctor at the deanery, and there married.

[italic: Enter CAIUS.]

[strong: Caius.] Vere is Mistress Page? By gar, I am cozened: I ha’ married un garçon, a boy; un paysan, by gar, a boy; it is not Anne Page: by gar, I am cozened.

[strong: Mrs Page.] Why, did you take her in green?

@style verse
  [strong: Caius.] Ay, by gar, and ’tis a boy: by gar, I’ll raise all
  Windsor. \[[italic: Exit.]

[strong: Ford.] This is strange. Who hath got the right Anne?

@style verse
  [strong: Page.] My heart misgives me:—here comes Master
  Fenton.

[italic: Enter FENTON and ANNE PAGE.]

How now, Master Fenton!

[strong: Anne.] Pardon, good father! good my mother, pardon!

@style verse
  [strong: Page.] Now, mistress, how chance you went not with
  Master Slender?

[strong: Mrs Page.] Why went you not with master doctor, maid?

@style verse
  [strong: Fent.] You do amaze her: hear the truth of it.
  You would have married her most shamefully,
  Where there was no proportion held in love.
  The truth is, she and I, long since contracted,
  Are now so sure that nothing can dissolve us.
  The offence is holy that she hath committed;
  And this deceit loses the name of craft,
  Of disobedience, or unduteous title;
  Since therein she doth evitate and shun
  A thousand irreligious cursed hours,
  Which forced marriage would have brought upon her.

@style verse
  [strong: Ford.] Stand not amazed; here is no remedy:
  In love the heavens themselves do guide the state;
  Money buys lands, and wives are sold by fate.

[strong: Fal.] I am glad, though you have ta’en a special stand to strike at me, that your arrow hath glanced.

@style verse
  [strong: Page.] Well, what remedy? Fenton, heaven give thee joy!
  What cannot be eschew’d must be embraced.

[strong: Fal.] When night-dogs run, all sorts of deer are chased.

@style verse
  [strong: Mrs Page.] Well, I will muse no further. Master Fenton,
  Heaven give you many, many merry days!
  Good husband, let us every one go home,
  And laugh this sport o’er by a country fire;
  Sir John and all.

@style verse
  [strong: Ford.] Let it be so. Sir John,
  To Master Brook you yet shall hold your word;
  For he to-night shall lie with Mistress Ford. \[[italic: Exeunt.]

NOTES: V, 5

